SOURCE NOTE: split out of B-2026-07-29-14 when the flattening half landed. That fix cleared the single-alias legs; this is what remains on the BOTH-aliased shape, and it is not an alias bug at heart.

REPRO (two-module package, `karac build`):

  // src/doer.kara
  pub trait Doer { fn go(ref self) -> i64; }
  pub struct Impl { pub n: i64 }
  impl Doer for Impl { fn go(ref self) -> i64 { self.n } }

  // src/main.kara
  import doer.{Impl as Widget, Doer as D};
  fn run[T: D](t: ref T) -> i64 { t.go() }
  fn main() { let w = Widget { n: 7 }; println(run(w).to_string()); }

  -> error[E0200]: trait bound `T: D` is not satisfied; `Widget` does not implement `D`

MEASURED, not inferred. An `eprintln!` inside `Env::type_satisfies_trait` shows both alias retries firing exactly as designed:

  (Widget, D) --trait_alias_canonical--> (Widget, Doer)
              --type_alias_canonical---> (Impl,   Doer)

and then `has_impl("Doer", "Impl", [])` returning FALSE. Dumping `env.impls` at that point shows ~300 entries, ALL of them stdlib (Option, Tensor, the primitive Add/Ord/From families, …) and not one from `doer.kara`. The canonicalization is correct; the table it consults is empty of the user impl.

WHY THE OTHER LEGS PASS: with the type spelled canonically the bound is discharged before the impl-table lookup is reached, so the missing entry never surfaces. That is why B-2026-07-29-10 could close on the trait retry alone and why only the both-aliased shape exposes this.

RELATED PRIOR WORK: B-2026-07-08-20 pulled an imported type's impl blocks into the per-module env for ASSOCIATED-FUNCTION lookup (`tests/cli.rs::test_build_project_resolves_imported_type_associated_fn`). This looks like the same gap on the TRAIT-IMPL axis — that fix likely registered the inherent impls only. Start there.

=== RESOLUTION 2026-07-29 ===

ROOT CAUSE was one line in `collect_import_origins`: the imported-impl pull-in (added by B-2026-07-08-20) was gated to NON-ALIASED imports —

    if ii.alias.is_none() || ii.alias.as_deref() == Some(origin_name) { … }

with the stated reasoning "an aliased type is re-bound under a new name while the impl's target / method signatures still name the origin, so registering it as-is would be inconsistent (left as a known limitation)".

That reasoning holds for the STRUCT re-binding a few lines above, which rewrites `local_def.name = bound_name`. It is BACKWARDS for the impl table, which is keyed by the type's own name and reached through `Env::type_alias_canonical`'s alias -> origin retry: registering the impl UNCHANGED, under the origin name, is precisely what that retry expects. Skipping it is what left the table empty.

FIX: collect for aliased imports too, registering TWO copies — the impl as-is (origin-keyed, for bound satisfaction) and a re-targeted twin under the alias (for method dispatch on an alias-typed value, since the struct is registered only under the bound name). The twin is skipped when the names coincide, which would register the same impl twice and read as a coherence conflict.

VERIFIED on a two-module package via `karac build`: both-aliased bound discharge, method call on an alias-typed value, and associated-fn dispatch on an alias all build and run correctly, alongside the no-alias control.

