WHY IT WAS LEFT: a local binding can legitimately shadow the alias name, so rewriting every bare `Identifier` that matches would silently redirect a VARIABLE reference — turning a rejection into a miscompile. A correct fix needs either scope-aware rewriting (skip names bound by an enclosing let / param / closure param / pattern) or a narrower gate such as callee-position only, which still has the shadowing hazard for a closure binding of the same name.

SEVERITY low: aliasing a function is rarer than aliasing a type, and the failure is a loud resolve error at build time, not a wrong answer.

=== RESOLUTION 2026-07-29 ===

FIX: bare `Identifier` nodes naming an aliased item are rewritten too. What makes that sound — and what the entry flagged as the blocker — is a widened shadow guard: `alias_subst_for_module` now drops any alias the module binds as a VALUE anywhere. `bound_value_names` collects parameters, `let` / `let else` / `let uninit` bindings, closure params, and match / if-let / while-let / for patterns across every function, impl method, trait default body, test case, const and module binding.

If the module never binds the name, an identifier spelling it cannot be a local, parameter, closure param or pattern binding — so the rewrite cannot redirect a variable reference, which was the failure mode worth avoiding.

Deliberately a CONSERVATIVE over-approximation: one binding anywhere in the module disables that alias's rewrite for the whole module, rather than tracking scopes. That costs nothing — those programs were rejected outright before — and keeps the pass free of scope-analysis bugs.

VERIFIED: `import m.{mk as build}` + `build(9)` builds and runs; a sibling program with `let build: i64 = 42` and a parameter named `build` keeps both locals (prints 42 and 5, not the imported fn). `tests/cli.rs::test_build_project_alias_rewrite_yields_to_a_shadowing_local` pins the guard by RUNNING the binary, not just building it.

