resolution:
FIXED, and the fix also settles this entry's SECOND, UNEXPLAINED INSTANCE — the two turned out to be one defect.

ROOT CAUSE. `rc_candidates` / `first_witness` (src/rc_predicate.rs) implement the formal predicate from design.md § Part 4 verbatim: a binding needs RC when some consume C and some other use U are DOMINANCE-INCOMPARABLE (¬dom(C,U) ∧ ¬dom(U,C)). That module's own header calls this a *necessary* condition, and that is exactly right — it is not a sufficient one. Two blocks are incomparable whenever neither precedes the other on ANY path, which includes the case where they cannot both execute at all. Mutually exclusive `if`/`else` arms are precisely that shape, so the predicate fired on code that consumes the value exactly once per path.

THE FIX is one added suppression in `first_witness`: skip the (C, U) pair when U is not forward-CFG-reachable from C. RC fallback for trigger 1 means "re-use AFTER consume", so what matters is whether U can execute after C — not whether the blocks are dominance-comparable. If no CFG path runs from C to U, no execution uses the value after consuming it.

IT IS DIRECTIONAL, AND THAT IS WHAT CLOSES THE SECOND INSTANCE. An earlier draft suppressed only on MUTUAL unreachability, which fixes the `if`/`else` rows but leaves `inner` reporting. The `inner` shape is not mutually exclusive at all — C IS reachable from U — but U still never runs after C:
    while i < 64 { inner.push(..) }   // U, every iteration
    let d = sha256(inner);            // C, once, after the loop
The loop body does not DOMINATE the post-loop consume (the CFG admits zero iterations), so dominance called them incomparable and fired — reporting an "other use" that PRECEDES the consume in source order, which is what made that report unreadable. One directional check covers both rows.

WHY THE ENTRY'S REDUCTIONS ALL CAME OUT CLEAN, which is worth recording because it cost real time: the second instance needs a LOOP AFTER THE CONSUME. With straight-line code after it the blocks collapse enough for dominance to hold and nothing fires — which is why "a vec built in a while loop and then consumed" was clean on its own. In `hmac_sha256` the trailing loop is `for b in inner_digest { outer.push(b) }`. Minimal repro:
    fn f() -> i64 {
        let mut inner: Vec[u8] = Vec.new();
        let mut i: i64 = 0;
        while i < 64 { inner.push(0u8); i = i + 1; }
        let d = sha256(inner);
        for b in d { i = i + (b as i64); }
        i
    }

WHAT MUST KEEP FIRING, and does — each has a test:
  * the canonical trigger-1 shape, `if b { takes(l) } … use(l)` after the join: U is reachable from C, so the pair survives;
  * THE SAME TWO ARMS INSIDE A LOOP: the back-edge makes each arm reachable from the other, so iteration 1's consume really can precede iteration 2's use. This is the reason the check is CFG reachability rather than a syntactic "same if/else" test, which would have been unsound here;
  * a sequential double consume: dominance-COMPARABLE, so it never reaches this code and is still reported as use-after-move, not RC.

DELIBERATE EXCLUSIONS. The suppression is gated to `ConsumeOrigin::Direct` and to blocks outside `closure_body_blocks`, for the same reason the neighbouring terminal-`return` case (B-2026-07-11-9) is: a closure body runs at its invocation, which CFG edges do not model, so reachability carries no information about whether the two sites can both execute. ClosureCapture and ContainerStore triggers keep today's behaviour untouched. The terminal-`return` case is now a strict special case of this one (a block whose only successor is exit reaches nothing) but was LEFT IN PLACE rather than folded in — it is separately gated and separately tested, and keeping the new check purely additive means it can only ever suppress, never re-fire something already suppressed.

MEASURED MATRIX, re-run after the fix (compare against this entry's own):
  if/else, both arms consume          -> clean   (was RC FALLBACK)
  match, both arms consume            -> clean   (was RC FALLBACK)
  only one arm consumes               -> clean   (unchanged)
  early-return then consume           -> clean   (unchanged)
  sequential double consume           -> warning[ownership] moved/used again (unchanged)
  consume on one arm + use after join -> RC FALLBACK (unchanged, genuine)
  two arms inside a loop              -> RC FALLBACK (unchanged, genuine)

DOGFOOD. `examples/sha256.kara` reported all three notes with its `#[allow(rc_fallback)]` attributes stripped and now reports none, so BOTH allows and their explanatory comments are removed; the Kāra code itself is unchanged and all NIST / RFC 4231 vectors still verify.

GATES (tests/ownership.rs): `mutually_exclusive_arms_consuming_once_each_no_rc_fallback` (if/else and match spellings) and `use_before_consume_with_a_later_loop_no_rc_fallback` — both verified to FAIL on the pre-fix compiler — plus `branch_arms_inside_a_loop_still_rc_fallback` and `consume_on_one_arm_with_use_after_join_still_rc_fallback`, which stay green on both sides and are what a too-aggressive version of this suppression would break. `tests/rc_predicate_parity.rs` (381) and the rest of tests/ownership.rs stay green.

detail:
Found while writing `examples/shortener` (a URL shortener) through the Mend loop. Its `/stats` route reported `{"links":2,"hits":{}}` — `LINKS.len()` was correct at 2, but `for (code, n) in HITS` over the sibling module-level Map yielded NOTHING. No crash, no diagnostic, just an empty result.

MINIMAL REPRO — the loop body never runs while every other access path on the SAME binding is correct:

    let mut MV: Vec[i64] = Vec.new();
    fn main() {
        MV.push(10); MV.push(20);
        let mut direct = 0;  for x in MV { direct = direct + x; }
        let mut indexed = 0; for i in 0..MV.len() { indexed = indexed + MV[i]; }
        let copy = MV.clone();
        let mut cloned = 0;  for x in copy { cloned = cloned + x; }
        println(direct);   // 0   <-- WRONG
        println(indexed);  // 30
        println(cloned);   // 30
    }

BLAST RADIUS. Vec, Map, Set AND String at module level (all five container arms share the dispatch). `karac build` (AOT) was wrong; `karac run --interp` was CORRECT — a silent run-vs-build divergence, the worst class, because the interpreter oracle disagrees with the shipped binary and nothing reports it.

ROOT CAUSE. `src/codegen/control_flow_for.rs`'s `compile_for` `ExprKind::Identifier` arm wrapped its ENTIRE container dispatch — String, Vec, Slice, Map, Set — inside `if let Some(slot) = self.variables.get(name)`, the LOCALS table. A module-level binding lives in `self.module_bindings`, not `self.variables`, so the `if let` body was skipped wholesale and control reached the arm's trailing `Ok(const_int(0))`. That constant is the arm's 'not an iterable I recognize' value, so the loop simply did not execute — a ZERO-ITERATION loop rather than an error.

Only the two ARRAY arms actually needed the locals table (they read `slot.ty`); the other five need only the NAME, because each `compile_for_*_var` helper recovers storage through `get_data_ptr`, which has had a `module_bindings` fallback all along, and the type side-tables (`vec_elem_types` / `map_key_types` / `set_elem_types`) are reseeded for module bindings by `reseed_module_binding_side_tables`. `compile_for_map_var` and `compile_for_set_var` even carry the comment "No `self.variables` precheck: `get_data_ptr` below gates" — the CALLER was imposing exactly the precheck the callees were written to avoid.

FIX. Split the arm: the array arms stay gated on `self.variables`; the five container arms now run when the name is in `self.variables` OR `self.module_bindings`. The gate is kept (rather than dropped entirely) so a name present in a type side-table but backed by neither storage still falls through instead of `unwrap()`-panicking in `get_data_ptr`.

WHY IT SURVIVED. Module-level containers are the normal way to hold service state — the `todo-api` kata uses `let mut TODOS: Vec[Todo]` — but that kata reaches its state through indexed loops and `.len()`, which were always correct. The bug needed a program that ITERATES module state directly, which is what `/stats` does. This file's other dispatch sites carry repeated warnings about the 'silent `_ =>` fall-through' 0-iteration failure mode; the same hazard sat in the Identifier arm itself, hidden behind a locals-only lookup rather than a missing match arm.

REGRESSION COVER: `tests/codegen.rs` — `test_e2e_modbind_container_for_loop_iterates` (Vec+Map+Set sums/counts), `..._matches_other_access_paths` (direct vs indexed vs clone all 30 — the discriminator that localized it), `..._string_for_loop_iterates_chars` (pins the String arm's ordering ahead of Vec, since String vars are also registered in `vec_elem_types` with an i8 element type).

