= String.new(); s.push_str(..); return Sloc { msg: s };` (b232/p1a) ABORTS, and `struct Sdir { msg: String }` built as `return Sdir { msg: f"payload-{i}" };` (b232/p1b) is CLEAN. One is a LOCAL moved into the struct literal; the other is built in place. That is the whole difference, and it explains every earlier reading: `W4 { a: i64, msg: String }` was inline (clean) and `W3 { a: i64, v: Vec[i64] }` was local (abort).  IT NEEDS ALL THREE CONDITIONS, each isolated by a clean control that keeps the local-into-literal construction and varies one thing:    q1 -- returned DIRECTLY, no carrier at all -- clean.    q2 -- through `Option` instead of `Result` -- clean.    q3 -- through `Result`, but a WILDCARD arm -- clean.    q4 -- through `Result`, binding arm, but a NAMED scrutinee -- clean.    p1a -- through `Result`, binding arm, FRESH-TEMP scrutinee -- ABORTS.  So: fresh-temp `Result` scrutinee AND an arm that binds AND a payload whose heap was moved in from a local. Drop any one and it is clean.  WHAT THAT REPOINTS THE FIX AT. This is the memory half of the "container moved into a struct-literal field keeps its source armed" family -- B-2026-08-02-20 -- reached through a `Result` payload, NOT a match-arm classification problem and NOT anything to do with `.len()` or the len-accessor intercept. Read B-2026-08-02-20's fix first and check why its suppression does not reach a local moved into a literal that becomes an inline `Result` payload.  STILL UNEXPLAINED, and the one thing left to measure: instrumentation shows p1a and p1b register IDENTICALLY in `main` -- same `FreeInlineResultPayload` overlay, same single `__karac_drop_struct_*` call, and the emitted IR for the two `__karac_drop_struct_*` bodies differs only in the element-size multiplier (`cap * 1` vs `cap * 8`). Both `main`s even emit the `respl.suppress.at.w1..w5` payload-area zeroing. So two owners are armed in BOTH, and p1b survives because one of the two frees finds `cap == 0` at runtime. Find out WHICH, and why the local-built value's cap survives where the inline-built one's does not -- that is the last step, and it is a runtime/IR question, not another probe.  SEVERITY UNCHANGED (high): SIGABRT (SIGSEGV for a `Vec[String]` field) with `karac run` correct, on a shape as ordinary as building an error struct field-by-field before returning it.  MINIMAL REPRO REACHED (2026-08-04, probes b232/r1..r8, s1, s2). Twelve lines, and a CLEAN control one token away:  
    struct Sx { msg: String }
    fn mkx(i: i64) -> Sx {
        let mut s: String = String.new();
        s.push_str(f"{i}");          // <-- r8: ABORTS
