 -- FIXED 2026-08-12 in 9f4fc14. Three of the four were pure registration gaps; the fourth was not a gap at all. -- `Set.clear()`: the row measured typecheck-vs-codegen and concluded codegen had it. True, but THE INTERPRETER DID NOT — it died with "method 'clear' not found on type 'Set' (no interpreter dispatch arm)". Registering the method alone would therefore have converted a check-time rejection into a RUN-VS-BUILD SPLIT: compiled fine, `karac run --interp` dead at runtime. The interpreter arm is added with the same element-drop discipline the Map/SortedMap arms already use (B-2026-08-03-2): snapshot the elements, empty the receiver, then run each element's user Drop body, so no borrow of the receiver is live while user code runs. GENERAL POINT: "codegen implements it" is not the same as "the backends implement it", and a two-way comparison can hide the third. -- `Column.range()` / `Tensor.range()`: registered at both reduce dispatch sites, element-typed exactly like `min`/`max` (which is what the two emitters subtract). Both backends already had it. Worth correcting the row's phrasing: this is the baked `Reduce[T]::range` DEFAULT, i.e. the METHOD `c.range()` returning `max - min`, not a `Column.range(lo, hi)` constructor — the tracker line reads like the latter and sent the first repro attempt the wrong way. -- `Vec.iter_mut()`: registered as yielding `mut ref T`. Both backends special-case it in FOR-LOOP position (codegen control_flow_for.rs, interpreter eval_expr.rs) and lowering.rs already documented the intended typing in prose — "Mutable iteration is the explicit `.iter_mut()` path (`for x in xs.iter_mut()` → `mut ref T`)" — so the design was settled and only the registration was missing. Typed for EVERY element type, matching the interpreter; codegen supports scalar elements and bails LOUD for heap elements / destructuring patterns with an `--interp` pointer and an index-loop suggestion. That asymmetry is deliberate and is the codebase's normal posture for a partially-lowered construct; it is strictly better than the previous state, where no shape was reachable at all. -- `.collect()` ON A DIRECT Vec IS NOT A GAP, and the row already suspected as much ("may be a deliberate rule the test violates"). It is: the diagnostic states the rule outright ("iterator adaptors/terminals require an explicit `.iter()`"). So the TEST PROGRAM was illegal, not the compiler. Rewritten as `.values().iter().collect()`, which keeps the receiver a MethodCall CHAIN and so preserves exactly what the original codegen dispatch bug (B-2026-07-08-17) was about. The simplest correct user code is plainer still — `let vs: Vec[i64] = m.values();` — since `collect` on an already-materialized Vec is identity. -- ALL FOUR REMOVED FROM CHECK_GATE_GRANDFATHERED, which empties the "REAL FRONT-END GAP" population that list was created to track; what remains is the deliberate codegen-layer negative test. Each of the four now passes with the check gate ENFORCING it, so the tests prove reachability and not just emitter behaviour. -- ADJACENT GAP FOUND AND DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN: `SortedSet.clear()` is missing from the typechecker too. An interpreter arm for it was written and then REMOVED, because codegen's SortedSet dispatch was not confirmed to implement clear — registering it unverified would have traded this bug for a checks-clean-then-fails-at-build one, which is the exact anti-pattern B-2026-08-11-23 was about. Worth a row for whoever confirms the codegen side. -- VERIFIED on interp / LLJIT / AOT for all four.

FOUND BY a word-frequency probe (tally into `Map[String, i64]`, then rank into a `Vec[Pair]` sorted by count) — i.e. by the swap at the heart of ANY hand-written sort, which is what makes this worth a high severity despite the narrow shape. The interpreter printed the full correct ranking; the native binary aborted with `free(): double free detected in tcache 2` and the JIT with a `ptr::copy_nonoverlapping` UB-precondition panic.

MINIMAL REPRO (10 lines, aborts on a default `karac build`):

