BOUNDARY, measured one variant at a time — every row below is the same program with one thing changed:

  read two elements, no assign   (`let a = ps[0]; let b = ps[1];`)      -- OK
  element struct has NO heap field (`struct Pair { m: i64, n: i64 }`)   -- OK
  RHS is a FRESH struct literal   (`ps[0] = Pair { … }`)                -- OK
  `Vec[String]` element copy      (`let b = xs[1]; xs[0] = b;`)         -- OK
  FIELD assign from the copy      (`ps[0].word = b.word;`)              -- OK
  RHS rebuilt field-wise          (`Pair { word: ps[1].word, … }`)      -- OK
  WHOLE-element copy assigned     (`let b = ps[1]; ps[0] = b;`)         -- ABORTS

So the trigger is precisely: element type is a user struct WITH a heap field, and the RHS of the index-assign is a binding holding a whole-element copy read out of a Vec. Both a fresh literal and a field-wise rebuild are safe, which is what pins the mechanism on the whole-element READ rather than on the store.

LIKELY MECHANISM (not yet confirmed in codegen): `let b = ps[1]` yields a shallow struct copy sharing the String buffer — safe on its own, since the binding's cleanup is evidently suppressed (that is the `let w = v[i]` case B-2026-06-14-11 / -12 fixed). Storing that shallow copy into another slot then gives the CONTAINER two elements holding one buffer pointer, and the Vec's per-element drop frees it twice at scope exit. `emit_displaced_index_elem_drop` correctly frees the slot's OLD occupant; nothing deep-copies the incoming aliasing RHS. The existing move-source suppression at that site (src/codegen/stmts.rs, `target_owns_heap_vec_elem`) is gated on a {ptr,len,cap} element type, so a user struct with a heap FIELD is not covered by it — which is consistent with `Vec[String]` being safe and `Vec[<struct with String>]` not.

