    enum Cmd { Insert(i64, String), Delete(i64, String) }
    struct Doc { lines: Vec[String] }
    fn apply(d: mut ref Doc, c: Cmd) -> Cmd {
        match c {
            Insert(at, text) => { d.lines.insert(at as usize, text); Cmd.Delete(at, d.lines[at as usize]) }
            Delete(at, _text) => { let gone = d.lines[at as usize]; d.lines.remove(at as usize); Cmd.Insert(at, gone) }
        }
    }
    fn main() {
        let mut l: Vec[String] = Vec.new();
        let mut a = String.new(); a.push_str("alpha");
        let mut b = String.new(); b.push_str("beta");
        l.push(a); l.push(b);
        let mut d = Doc { lines: l };
        let inv = apply(mut d, Cmd.Delete(1, "beta"));   // Vec element -> enum payload
        let i2  = apply(mut d, inv);                     // enum payload -> Vec, then read out again
        println(f"{d.lines.len()}");
    }

    karac check          no errors
    karac run --interp   2                      (correct)
    karac run (JIT)      free(): double free detected in tcache 2
    karac build (AOT)    free(): double free detected in tcache 2
    KARAC_OPT_LEVEL=0    same        KARAC_AUTO_PAR=0   same

NECESSARY CONDITIONS -- each has a measured clean control:

    variant                                                              verdict
    baseline (above)                                                     ABORT
    only ONE `apply` call (either arm alone)                             ok
    Insert arm returns `Cmd.Delete(at, "sentinel")` instead of re-reading ok
    Delete arm reuses its own payload instead of reading the Vec         ok
    payloads are string LITERALS rather than heap-built                  ok   (see hazard note)
    the same round trip with NO enum, three forms:
      inline in main (`let g = l[1]; l.remove(1); l.insert(1,g); let h = l[1]`)   ok
      the same through a `Doc` struct                                    ok
      the same split across two `mut ref` helper fns returning String    ok

So it needs, together: (1) an enum with a heap payload; (2) a function taking that enum BY VALUE and RETURNING a new one; (3) one arm that reads a Vec element OUT into the returned variant, and another arm that moves a payload INTO the Vec and then reads an element out AGAIN into its returned variant -- BOTH reads, either alone is clean; (4) two chained calls so the value completes the loop Vec -> enum -> Vec -> enum.

The three no-enum controls are the useful part of the diagnosis: the plain data round trip through a `Vec` is already correct, including through a struct field and across two `mut ref` helpers. Only when the value travels inside an enum variant does an owner get duplicated. That points at the variant-construction path duplicating the element handle without either cap-zeroing the Vec slot or copying the buffer, rather than at anything in `Vec.insert` / `Vec.remove` / index-read.

Wrapper structs and stacks are NOT required and were stripped: the original find had an `Editor { doc, undo: Vec[Cmd], redo: Vec[Cmd] }` with push/pop stacks and a `while` loop, and all of it reduced away. An ignored `_text` binding is also not the cause -- using the payload instead of ignoring it still aborts.

MEASUREMENT HAZARD (same class as B-2026-08-04-17, and it bit here): with string LITERAL payloads the program runs clean, because a literal is static and the second free lands on a non-heap pointer. The first reduction attempt therefore looked like it had escaped the bug. Any fixture for this must build its payloads at run time (`String.new()` + `push_str`).

WHY IT IS HIGH: this is the standard undo/redo command shape -- a command enum carrying the text it moved, applied to a document, with the inverse handed back. The abort skips the stdout flush, so a program that prints 19 correct lines under `--interp` produces NO output at all when compiled, which reads as a startup failure rather than a fault at the second command.

