So the rule is narrow and mechanical: it is the CONTAINER family (`Vec`/`Map`/`Set`) on a `shared struct` specifically. Both discriminators have a working control -- `String` on the same shared struct is released correctly, and the same container on a plain struct is released correctly -- which is what makes this look like a missing arm rather than a missing mechanism.

IT LEAKS THE WHOLE CONTAINER, AND IT SCALES. A `Map` with one entry leaks 600 bytes in 3 allocations (control block plus buckets, not a fragment). A `Vec[String]` with three 11-byte elements leaks 129 bytes in 4 allocations -- the buffer plus each element -- so the loss tracks the container's contents, not a fixed header.

WHY IT MATTERS. Replacing a container field wholesale is the ordinary way to filter or reset one: build the new collection, assign it over the old. In the program this came from -- a directory tree of `shared struct Node` with `mut children: Vec[Node]` and a `weak parent` back-edge -- removing a subtree is exactly `root.children = kept;`, and that single statement leaked the entire discarded Vec. A long-running program that prunes repeatedly leaks on every prune while reporting correct sizes and counts throughout.

INVISIBLE OUTSIDE LSAN. The originating tree program's output was byte-identical and correct on all three backends against a hand-checked oracle -- recursive totals (540 bytes over 6 nodes), the weak-parent path walk (`/root/src/lib.rs`), a missing-node lookup, the root's `None` parent, and the post-removal recount (4 nodes / 460 bytes). The 3-backend differential cannot see this class.

