CAUSE. `record_runtime_error` returns `Value::Unit` and sets `pending_cf = Some(ControlFlow::RuntimeError)`, which is the interpreter's standard fault protocol -- roughly 125 sites read `pending_cf.is_some()` to short-circuit. The `ExprKind::Index` arm did not. It evaluated both operands, and when the INNER subscript had faulted it read the returned `Unit` as a real value, matched the pair `(Unit, Unit)` against every container arm, missed them all, and hit the catch-all `unreachable!()`.

So the `unreachable!()` was only ever sound for operand pairs produced by SUCCESSFUL evaluation, and nothing enforced that precondition.

NOT MAP-SPECIFIC, and this is the part worth recording: any nested subscript reproduces it, because the defect is in how the outer index treats a failed inner one, not in any container. A nested Vec with an out-of-range outer index -- `v[9][0]` on a `Vec[Vec[i64]]` -- ICE'd identically.

