SPLIT OUT of B-2026-08-18-39 rather than folded into it, because it is a DIFFERENT defect that row's fix merely made reachable. -39 was a use-after-free: the auto-par branch freed the boxed payload it had just published to the parent's return slot. Fixing it transfers ownership to the parent — and if the parent then MOVES the value into a call, the box is owned by nobody and leaks.

REPRO (auto-par; `karac build`, run under ASAN+LSan):
    struct W { f0: i64, f1: i64, f2: i64, f3: i64 }
    fn get(v: ref Vec[W], k: i64) -> Option[W] { ... return Some(v[i]); ... }
    fn tail(o: Option[W]) -> i64 { match o { Some(w) => w.f3, None => -1i64 } }
    fn build(seed: i64) -> i64 {
        let mut ns: Vec[W] = Vec.new();
        ns.push(W { f0: seed, f1: seed+1, f2: seed+2, f3: seed+3 });
        let hit = get(ns, seed);          // two independent lets -> forks
        let miss = get(ns, seed + 99i64);
        return tail(hit) + tail(miss);    // MOVES both
    }
    // main loops build() 2000x
    -> SUMMARY: AddressSanitizer: 64000 byte(s) leaked in 2000 allocation(s)

Swapping the consumer for a READING one (`hit?.f3`) is clean, which is what localises it to the move.

THE IR IS EXPLICIT about both halves. At the call site the caller emits
    %hit25 = load { i64, i64, i64, i64 }, ptr %hit23
    store { i64, i64, i64, i64 } zeroinitializer, ptr %hit23   ; move-out sentinel
    %call = call i64 @tail({ i64, i64, i64, i64 } %hit25)
and its scope-exit `BoxedEnumDrop` then reloads that slot, reads tag 0, and skips. `@tail` contains no `free` and no drop call at all — it recovers the payload with `inttoptr` (`%enumbox.mv`) and returns. So the sentinel hands ownership to a callee that never takes it.

WHY IT WAS NEVER SEEN SEQUENTIALLY, measured rather than assumed. The same source with `KARAC_AUTO_PAR=0` reports clean at 2000 iterations — but not because anything frees. `objdump` on the sequential binary shows NO user function calling `malloc`, while the auto-par binary has one in `__par_branch_0_0`; the ASAN size-class stats agree (2176 allocations in the 48-byte class under auto-par, none sequentially). Everything inlines into one function, the box never escapes, and LLVM promotes the allocation away entirely. Under auto-par the pointer crosses a thread boundary through the return struct, so it is a real allocation and the missing free becomes a real leak.

SO THE FIX IS ONE OF: make a by-value enum parameter adopt its boxed payload (the callee frees, which is what the sentinel already assumes), or suppress the caller's sentinel for a boxed-payload binding so the caller keeps ownership. The first matches the ownership model the move-out sentinel is written against; the second is smaller but makes `move` not mean move for this one shape. Deciding between them is the work.

SEVERITY MEDIUM, not high: it is a bounded leak on a shape that is correct in value, whereas its parent row was silent data corruption. Reachable today only through auto-par, where the allocation survives the optimizer.

MEASURED while closing B-2026-08-19-16, and deliberately NOT fixed with it: that row was the QUALIFIED path (`First.A`), which is unambiguous and simply had no env binding. This is the BARE form, where the program is genuinely ambiguous and the two backends resolve it differently.

REPRO:

    enum First { A, B }
    enum Second { A, C }
    impl First { fn tag(ref self) -> i64 { 1 } }
    impl Second { fn tag(ref self) -> i64 { 2 } }
    fn main() { let x = A; println(x.tag()); }

`karac build` prints 1. `karac run` prints 2. `karac check` reports "All checks passed."

WHICH ENUM EACH SIDE PICKS. The interpreter takes the LAST DECLARED: `register_items` writes `env["A"]` once per enum and the later write wins. The typechecker does NOT follow declaration order -- with `First` first it picks `First`, and with the order REVERSED (`Second` declared first) it still picks `First`; renaming to `Zebra` (first) and `Alpha` (second) makes it pick `Alpha`. All three data points are consistent with the alphabetically-first enum name, i.e. a sorted or otherwise name-ordered map rather than a deliberate rule. Deterministic across repeated runs (checked 5x), so this is not hash-order nondeterminism -- but it is not a rule anyone chose either.

WHY IT IS FILED RATHER THAN FIXED. There are three defensible answers and the spec picks none of them: (a) reject a bare ambiguous variant at resolve with a diagnostic naming both enums and suggesting the qualified form, (b) resolve it type-directed from the expected type, so `want_second(A)` means `Second.A`, or (c) keep an arbitrary winner and merely make the backends agree. (c) is the cheapest and the worst -- it cements a name-ordering accident as language semantics. (a) is the smallest honest fix and matches the language's existing preference for explicitness; it is also a BREAKING change for any program relying on today's bare form with a collision. Making that call is the work here, and it belongs to whoever owns the surface, not to the session that found it.

NOTE: with NO collision, the bare form is fine and must keep working -- the pattern matcher classifies a bare PascalCase identifier as a unit-variant pattern only when `env.get(name)` returns a unit `EnumVariant`, and breaking that reopens B-2026-06-30-14. Any fix has to leave the unambiguous case untouched; tests/interpreter.rs::bare_unit_variant_still_resolves_when_unambiguous pins it.

CORRECTION (2026-08-19, at close) -- to this row's own characterisation of the existing code. The row says the typechecker's pick "tracks neither declaration order nor anything the spec names -- consistent with the alphabetically-first enum name, which is a name-ordering accident rather than a rule". The first half is accurate; the last clause is not fair to the code, and is corrected here.

The sorted scan is DELIBERATE and load-bearing. B-2026-08-14-10 replaced a `HashMap`-iteration scan whose order came from a per-process `RandomState`, so a bare `None` typed as `Option[T]` on some runs and as the user enum on others -- `karac check` returning a coin flip on identical bytes, measured 15/30, 10/20 and 7/16. The fix scans a SORTED key list in two tiers, user-declared enums before stdlib/prelude, with `builtin_variant_owner` pinning `Some`/`None`/`Ok`/`Err` to the builtin. Both the determinism and the tiering were reasoned about and are documented at the site.

What was NEVER chosen is only the USER-vs-USER tie-break, which falls out as alphabetical because sorting was the determinism fix and the question of two user enums colliding was not what that row was about. That narrow gap is what this row fixes -- not the scan, which was right.

The genuinely mis-stated part of the original diagnosis was elsewhere: the row framed this as "the rule is arbitrary" when the primary defect was that the INTERPRETER never implemented the rule at all. Typechecker and codegen already agreed; one backend was missing. That reframing is why the fix is part (a) first -- a straight compliance bug with no language change attached -- and part (b) second.

NOT INVESTIGATED, and worth someone's time later: whether type-directed resolution (accepting `let x: Second = A;` and `want_second(A)` by using the expected type) is wanted. It is strictly more expressive than either the old silent pick or the new rejection, and (b) is deliberately shaped so it can be added later as a pure relaxation.

